{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788263968,"nonce":"uD9VSvG78R7npV5Fn5Zzfk3Y9lc=","title":"A dead rwa client leaves the game RUNNING: the mod has no client liveness at all","message":"Finding G (mod half), from M1 run `m1-20260831`. The client half is being fixed separately; this issue is the mod's.\n\n## What happened\n\nAn `rwa advance` whose CLIENT process died left the game advancing at `Ultrafast` with nobody watching. `rwa pause`, issued later by hand, reported `was_advancing: true, speed_before: Ultrafast`.\n\nMeasured consequence: **~60,000 ticks — a full in-game day — elapsed unobserved between the day-3 read and the next advance, and it happened more than once.** The fingerprints are `transcripts/m1-20260831/136-advance` and `187-advance`, both empty directories.\n\nSession 12 first blamed a stray keypress on the watched window and corrected that in its ledger. The corrected cause is client death.\n\n## Root cause, at source\n\n**The mod has no client-\u003emod liveness of any kind. The heartbeat runs one way** — the mod reports, the client listens, and nothing tells the mod that the far end has gone. An advance is started by a command and is stopped only by another command, so a client that never sends the next command leaves the game ticking indefinitely.\n\nThe client-side aggravator (being fixed on its own branch): `rwa` mkdirs the step directory BEFORE sending and writes both `cmd.json` and `result.json` only AFTER the result returns — so when the client dies mid-call an empty directory is the only trace, and the transcript cannot even say what was in flight.\n\n## Why this is the mod's problem too\n\nFixing the client's write order makes the death legible after the fact. It does not stop the game. An unattended install advancing at Ultrafast past the end of its supervision is the failure mode that makes a long unattended run unauditable — the journal keeps recording, but nothing in it is a decision anyone made.\n\n## Scope\n\nDesign the mod-side dead-man switch. Not pre-decided; the investigation is the work:\n\n- What is the liveness signal? Candidates: the client touching a heartbeat file on an interval the mod polls; the mod requiring an explicit keepalive to continue past N ticks of a long advance; the existing `Poller` noticing its request directory has gone stale.\n- What is the response? Almost certainly `pause` plus a journalled event naming the reason, so the gap is visible in the record rather than inferred from a tick jump. Do NOT silently resume.\n- What is the threshold, and is it configurable via `Config.cs`? It must not fire during a legitimately long single advance.\n- Interaction with `TimeDriver`'s existing cap arithmetic — the switch must not fight the cap or double-pause.\n\nConstraint: the file half of the bridge never touches Verse (analyzerbridge is the template); all Verse access on the main thread at a safe point.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- With the client killed mid-advance, the game pauses within the configured threshold instead of running on.\n- The pause is journalled as its own event, naming client-loss as the cause and carrying the tick at which supervision was lost.\n- A legitimately long advance under a live client does NOT trip the switch. Prove both directions.\n- The threshold is configurable and its default is stated with the reasoning for that number.\n- `accept/4.2-play-loop.py` can tell a client-loss pause from an ordinary one.","files":null}]}